From 72572f7b1ffab5bf4e10c6d879b08fba19d84baf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 07:58:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Build pricing, cart, checkout and the payment providers
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Implements the pricing model as specified: four tables in two layers, with
a customer's negotiated rate always beating the list price, and nothing
priced showing as quoted individually rather than zero.

Every price row carries its own currency, and the minor-unit divisor comes
from Intl rather than a hardcoded 100 — a currency without decimals
formats correctly rather than being divided by 100.

Order totals are deliberately not frozen. Each line keeps the key it was
bought under and the total is re-derived on display, so a catalogue
correction reaches existing orders. Paid orders are never repriced, orders
that don't reference exactly one item are skipped, an item that can no
longer be priced is left alone rather than zeroed, and only figures that
actually moved are written — in one statement, however long the list.

The customer-price endpoint derives the user from the session cookie and
never from the request body.

Payments sit behind one interface. Razorpay verifies the callback HMAC and
PayPal captures server-side; neither trusts what the browser posts back.
With no credentials configured, checkout still records the order and says
plainly that payment isn't live.

Migrations are squashed to a single baseline: the old speculative pricing
tables are gone, and money columns are now *_minor rather than *_pence.
Recreate the development database rather than migrating onto this.

Two things the tests turned up: the price endpoint validated the request
body only after the session check, so a malformed body from a signed-out
caller returned 200; and adding to the cart gave no feedback at all.

Covered by 26 unit tests and 42 end-to-end tests.

